is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated RIP across the core and edge routers (router1, router2, router3, plus adjacent routers 0/4/5/6). The RIP control-plane and routing tables appear stable, with no evidence of adjacency bouncing or route aging/relearning every few seconds.

Mechanism and evidence:
- On router1/router2/router3, “show ip route” displays multiple RIP-learned inter-site routes (e.g., 192.168.15.0/24, 192.168.200.0/24, 172.25.1.0/24) with monotonically increasing ages around 00:06:30+ when sampled repeatedly; no timers reset during multi-second sampling windows, and no entries disappeared/reappeared.
- On routers 0/4/5/6, “show ip rip” shows consistent RIP entries with stable ages, matching the core’s view.
- Physical links on the suspected core path (router2-eth4 <-> router3-eth4, the 192.168.100.0/24 segment) are up and error-free: ip -s link shows no errors/drops; ethtool reports link detected; dmesg shows no link flaps.

Localization:
- Core inter-site segments (r2–r3 on 192.168.100.0/24; r1’s WAN sides 10.16.1.0/24 and 10.20.1.0/24) show stable adjacencies and routes.

Root cause:
- No RIP instability observed during testing; any prior flapping could not be reproduced.

Corrective fix:
- No corrective action required at this time. If intermittent flaps recur, enable targeted logging (debug rip events/packets) on router1/router2/router3 and capture for a few minutes; also poll interface state counters on r2/r3 eth4 to correlate any future resets with link events. Default RIP timers and configurations presently look normal. 
=== END ===